    "intro": "A good bakery name is easy to say, easy to find and tells people what's in the window, whether that's sourdough, pastries or birthday bakes. A bakery name goes on the sign, the paper bags and the Instagram handle, so it needs to work in all three. This **bakery name generator** gives you names in eight styles, from punny to French, including ones built from your town or your own name.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Eight good bakery names",
        "content": "<p>All eight came from the generator:</p><ul><li><strong>Well Bread</strong>: a pun everyone gets on first reading.</li><li><strong>All You Knead</strong>: a song-title pun that tells you it's a bakery.</li><li><strong>The Bun Also Rises</strong>: a literary joke on Hemingway's <em>The Sun Also Rises</em>, for a bakery with a sense of humour.</li><li><strong>Scone But Not Forgotten</strong>: a funny name that works on a café blackboard.</li><li><strong>Little Rolling Pin</strong>: cute and homely, for a home bakery or a small shop.</li><li><strong>Pemberton & Son Bakers</strong>: a classic family name that sounds as if it's been on the high street for decades.</li><li><strong>Hearth & Peel</strong>: a modern pair of baking words: the hearth is the oven floor, and a peel is the flat shovel bakers slide loaves in with.</li><li><strong>Boulangerie du Moulin</strong>: French for \"the mill bakery\", for bread made the French way.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "How bakery names work",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Baking words make good names.</strong> <em>Proof</em> is the dough's final rise, <em>crumb</em> is the texture inside a loaf, and <em>knead</em>, <em>rye</em> and <em>crust</em> all lend themselves to puns.</li><li><strong>French names say pâtisserie.</strong> A <em>boulangerie</em> sells bread and a <em>pâtisserie</em> sells pastries and cakes. In France, <em>boulangerie</em> is protected: only bakers who knead, shape and bake their bread on the premises may use it. Elsewhere it simply sets expectations, so use it if your croissants can live up to it.</li><li><strong>Classic names borrow the street or the family:</strong> <em>Church Street Bakery</em>, <em>Whitaker &amp; Sons Bakers</em>. They suggest a bakery that has always been there.</li><li><strong>Town names help people find you.</strong> Someone searching \"bakery near me\" or \"[town] bakery\" will find <em>Hampton Baking Co.</em> more easily than a pun.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Check it's free before you print the sign",
        "content": "<p>A name from a generator is a starting point, not permission to use it. Before you order the sign, the boxes or the van livery:</p><ul><li><strong>Company register.</strong> In the UK, search Companies House; you can't register a limited company with the same name as an existing one. In the US, search your state's business register.</li><li><strong>Trade marks.</strong> Search the UK trade mark register (or the USPTO in the US) for the goods and services you'll sell.</li><li><strong>Domain and handles.</strong> Check the .co.uk or .com and the Instagram and TikTok handles you'll need.</li><li><strong>Your area.</strong> Search a map app for businesses with the same or a similar name nearby.</li></ul><p>Press <strong>Check</strong> on any name, or use the <a href=\"../business-name-checker.html\">Business Name Checker</a>, to run most of these at once. For the full process, read our guide, <a href=\"../guides/business-naming-best-practices.html\">Business Naming Best Practices</a>. This is general guidance, not legal advice.</p><p>In the UK, any food business, including a home bakery, must register with the local council at least 28 days before it starts trading. Registration is free, and the council will inspect you and give you a food hygiene rating.</p>"
